import Mathlib

/-!
# MathDB #356988 — backtracking PageRank monotonicity is FALSE

Write-up `mathdb-open-problems/problems/356988/solution.tex`,
*Two exact counterexamples to backtracking PageRank monotonicity*, 18 September 2026.

Conjecture 3.1 of the cited preprint asserts that every coordinate of the backtracking
PageRank `π_μ` is monotonic in the backtracking weight `μ`.  The witness is the five-vertex
**house graph** `E = {01, 03, 04, 12, 23, 34}` with uniform personalization and damping
`ε = 1/2`.  Following the source's Definition 2.4, the oriented-edge masses satisfy

`y_ij = (1−ε)/(n d_i) + ε/(d_i−1+μ) · (μ y_ji + ∑_{h∼i, h≠j} y_hi)`,

and the vertex PageRank is `π_μ(i) = ∑_{j∼i} y_ij`.  At the roof vertex 4,

`π(1/2) = 8356/47445  >  π(1) = 22/125  <  π(2) = 3134/17805`,

which is down and then up, so that coordinate is not monotonic.

## The obligation that is easy to miss

The conjecture is about **the** stationary vector, so exhibiting *a* solution of the
defining system would refute nothing.  Each `π` statement below is therefore universally
quantified over solutions — `∀ y, Aᵥ *ᵥ y = bᵥ → …` — and uniqueness is supplied by an
EXPLICIT inverse of the system matrix at each `μ`, checked by `decide`.  That is what makes
the refutation bind to the conjecture rather than to some solution of a linear system.

The graph also meets the source's standing hypotheses: simple, undirected, connected, and
minimum degree two (`deg = (3,2,2,3,2)`), so (1) is defined even at `μ = 0`.

Verified in exact rational arithmetic before formalizing, by solving the full
12-oriented-edge system from scratch rather than reusing the write-up's symmetry collapse:
all three values, total mass exactly 1, positivity of every mass, and
`π(1/2) − π(1) = 142/1186125`.
-/

